mtd: onenand: N8x0: Nokia's OTP user/factory split

Mainline splits a 512 Mbit+ OneNAND's OTP block after page 50: user
pages 0-49, factory 50-63. Nokia's N800/N810 kernel (2.6.21) split it
after page 10, and that is how these units were programmed: the CAL's
factory blocks (the WLAN MAC and BT address) are in the 20 KB user region
that Maemo's tools (DSME, the CAL) read with OTPSELECT. Use Nokia's
split on N8x0 boards, so the user region is the CAL's and nothing more,
and the factory region is what Nokia called factory.

--- a/drivers/mtd/nand/onenand/onenand_base.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/nand/onenand/onenand_base.c
@@ -26,6 +26,7 @@
 #include <linux/mtd/mtd.h>
 #include <linux/mtd/onenand.h>
 #include <linux/mtd/partitions.h>
+#include <linux/of.h>
 
 #include <asm/io.h>
 
@@ -3050,6 +3051,13 @@
 	density = onenand_get_density(this->device_id);
 	if (density < ONENAND_DEVICE_DENSITY_512Mb)
 		otp_pages = 20;
+	else if (of_machine_is_compatible("nokia,n8x0"))
+		/*
+		 * Nokia's N800/N810 kernel split the OTP block after page 10:
+		 * the CAL's factory blocks (WLAN MAC, BT address) are in those
+		 * user pages, and Maemo's tools size the user region by it
+		 */
+		otp_pages = 10;
 	else
 		otp_pages = 50;
 
